    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51787.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "I'm not sure that that extra 7,000 troops is going to change the calculus that the Ukrainian military intelligence are making at the moment. I saw their latest intelligence assessment earlier on today, and they were saying that, look, we estimate there are 148,000. More than 148 was the figure they gave. That puts it in line with what Joe Biden, President Biden said last night, 150. Obviously, that's been up now by another 7,000. But the point that Ukrainians are making in this intelligence assessment is that at that level of force concentration, that's not enough, they believe, for the Russians to stage a significant large-scale military attack, a full-scale invasion, for instance, inside Ukraine. It doesn't mean they can't do something smaller or even attempt it. with forces at that level. I think the other factor we have to consider though, when we talk about the number of forces that are available for any kind of Russian invasion, and we don't often talk about this, are the Russian back rebels in those",
