    "turn_text": "Well, I think the administration and NATO have done a really good job here. You know, we came in with a Franklin with a weak hand. We've been treating Putin the wrong way for years. He's been a war with us and we've continued to sort of accommodate him. You know, we came out of a sort of a real fumble in Afghanistan. Our internal politics look weak and a year ago in President Trump was in power. It looked like NATO might even fall apart. So what the administration has done is a variety of things, a layers of things to try to deter. They've talked about serious sanctions, they've moved troops to Europe, to NATO, to reinstrengthen it, to add unity. They've worked very, very hard on communicating. The president's speech yesterday was really good about trying to resease the narrative from Putin, because essentially Putin manufactured this crisis out of thin air. There was nothing, there was no threat to him, there was nothing, and he created this false narrative about being humiliated in the 90s, which frankly wasn't true. We tried to help Russia in the 90s about threats from Ukraine and from NATO in the United States, which aren't true. And the president pointed that out. So all of these things are just trying to push back, make clear that our allies are together and create a deterrent effect. So all of it, using intelligence, working together, sending troops to Europe. And I think the administration has done a good job of really marshaling resources here to make it, to know that we're serious.",
