    "turn_idx": 68,
    "turn_text": "Well, Aaron, it's been very clear from the start that what the committee is trying to uncover is a direct link between the former president's conduct leading up to January 6 and these attempts to overturn the will of the American voters. And what these White House visitor logs could show are the type of people that were coming in and out of the White House in the days leading up to January 6. And we already know from some of the public reporting that there were many of these individuals that were at the front lines of this attempt to undermine the election results. People like Rudy Giuliani, Sidney Powell, others like Congressman Mo Brooks and those who were peddling these false claims about the election results and even taking it a step further by scheming up different legal theories that could be used to try and prevent the certification of those election results. Now, while this information is important to the committee, what is unclear is just how much it will provide them in terms of piecing this puzzle together. First of all, we know the Trump administration was a little bit lackluster when it came to record keeping on a number of fronts. We've already reported about that as it relates to the call logs on the day of January 6. But there's also only so much information these visitor logs can tell you. It's basically just who gets cleared into the building on any given day. It doesn't necessarily say what offices they go to after their first initial meeting. And it also lacks a lot of detailed information about who comes in and out of the White House residents. So what we've seen though, Aaron, is that the committee is casting a wide net. They're talking to a lot of people. They've deposed a number of people. They're conducting a lot of interviews. This may not present everything that they're looking for, but they're hoping it's just another piece of information that will allow them to paint the picture of what happened on January 6.",
