    "turn_text": "Right, right. And as you say, so many pieces, right? I mean, even their interview piece of the puzzle already, you've got 550 plus interview puzzle pieces. So putting it all together to assemble the picture. Thank you. And next, the Russian figure skater who tested positive for a banned substance just became the first woman to land a quadruple jump at the Olympics. So next, I'm going to speak to a former U.S. Olympic skater. She was the first woman to land a triple axle. In Hela Viv, where I am tonight, marked what here in Ukraine was called a day of unity. Tonight, Russian figure skater Camila Valleava, just hours away from her final Olympic skate in these games, the 15-year-old is in first place right now, and she is allowed to compete despite testing positive for a banned substance before the games. Out of front now, Mariah Nagasu, former U.S. Olympic figure skater, she won bronze at the 2018 Games. Mariah, I really appreciate talking to you. You understand this better than anyone. How does it make you feel to see Valleava on the ice and leading the pack despite this positive test?",
